1
00:00:00,126 --> 00:00:09,433
(Q31) For my presentation today I want to tell you about how groups of elephants have been moved and settled in new reserves.

2
00:00:09,433 --> 00:00:17,363
This is known as translocation and has been carried out in Malawi in Africa in recent years.

3
00:00:17,363 --> 00:00:24,711
The reason this is being done is because of overpopulation of elephants in some areas.

4
00:00:24,711 --> 00:00:32,559
Overpopulation is a good problem to have and not one we tend to hear about very often.

5
00:00:32,559 --> 00:00:43,060
In Malawi’s Majete National Park the elephant population had been wiped out by poachers, who killed the elephants for their ivory.

6
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:51,446
But in 2003, the park was restocked and effective law enforcement was introduced.

7
00:00:51,446 --> 00:00:56,354
Since then, not a single elephant has been poached.

8
00:00:56,354 --> 00:01:01,946
In this safe environment, the elephant population boomed.

9
00:01:01,946 --> 00:01:09,231
Breeding went so well that there were more elephants than the park could support.

10
00:01:09,231 --> 00:01:12,241
[Q31小标题定位] This led to a number of problems.

11
00:01:12,241 --> 00:01:21,697
Firstly, there was more competition for food, which meant that some elephants were suffering from hunger.

12
00:01:21,697 --> 00:01:30,480
As there was a limit to the amount of food in the national park, some elephants began looking further afield.

13
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:41,884
[Q31答案句： knock down = damage] Elephants were routinely knocking down fences around the park, which then had to be repaired at a significant cost.

14
00:01:41,884 --> 00:01:55,184
(Q32) To solve this problem, the decision was made to move dozens of elephants from Majete National Park to Nkhotakota Wildlife Park, where there were no elephants.

15
00:01:55,184 --> 00:02:09,587
But, obviously, attempting to move significant numbers of elephants to a new home 300 kilometres away is quite a challenge.

16
00:02:09,587 --> 00:02:16,142
So how did this translocation process work in practice?

17
00:02:16,142 --> 00:02:25,281
[Q32答案句： same = one] Elephants were moved in groups of between eight and twenty, all belonging to one family.

18
00:02:25,281 --> 00:02:33,553
(Q33) Because relationships are very important to elephants, they all had to be moved at the same time.

19
00:02:33,553 --> 00:02:48,042
[Q33答案句： 空前是动词要预测替换， 最好再往前划一下名词，本题跟名词+flew over park in a ... = make use of] A team of vets and park rangers flew over the park in helicopters and targeted a group, which were rounded up and directed to a designated open plain.

20
00:02:48,042 --> 00:03:07,978
(Q34) The vets then used darts to immobilise the elephants – this was a tricky manoeuvre, as they not only had to select the right dose of tranquiliser for different-sized elephants but they had to dart the elephants as they were running around.

21
00:03:07,978 --> 00:03:16,382
[Q34答案句： minimize= reduce] This also had to be done as quickly as possible so as to minimise the stress caused.

22
00:03:16,382 --> 00:03:24,134
(Q35) As soon as the elephants began to flop onto the ground, the team moved in to take care of them.

23
00:03:24,134 --> 00:03:42,859
[Q35答案句，先提空后内容，作为「举例」，然后用so 连接词， 引出答案。] To avoid the risk of suffocation, the team had to make sure none of the elephants were lying on their chests because their lungs could be crushed in this position.So all the elephants had to be placed on their 【sides.】

24
00:03:42,859 --> 00:03:49,944
(Q36) One person stayed with each elephant while they waited for the vets to do checks.

25
00:03:49,944 --> 00:04:03,438
[Q36答案句： 空前所有格，可能有语序调换， 然后 keep an eye= 空后的monitor] It was very important to keep an eye on their breathing – if there were fewer than six breaths per minute, the elephant would need urgent medical attention.

26
00:04:03,438 --> 00:04:11,867
(Q37) Collars were fitted to the matriarch in each group so their movements could be tracked in their new home.

27
00:04:11,867 --> 00:04:25,393
[Q37答案句：tusks原词，   跟连接词 and also ,  出答案feet] Measurements were taken of each elephant’s tusks – elephants with large tusks would be at greater risk from poachers – and also of their feet.

28
00:04:25,393 --> 00:04:35,374
(Q38) The elephants were then taken to a recovery area before being loaded onto trucks and transported to their new home.

29
00:04:35,374 --> 00:05:00,492
[Q38答案句： 空前无实义词只有小标题， 先跟上。 然后 ... prospects= opportunity] The elephants translocated to Nkhotakota settled in very well and the project has generally been accepted to have been a huge success – and not just for the elephants.【Employment】 prospects have improved enormously, contributing to rising living standards for the whole community.

30
00:05:00,492 --> 00:05:17,499
(Q39) [Q39答案句： poachers原词， 然后跟 in fact ... give up ... = reduction in 。。。] Poaching is no longer an issue, as former poachers are able to find more reliable sources of income.In fact, many of them volunteered to give up their 【weapons】, as they were no longer of any use to them.

31
00:05:17,499 --> 00:05:24,883
(Q40) More than two dozen elephants have been born at Nkhotakota since relocation.

32
00:05:24,883 --> 00:05:34,548
With an area of more than 1,800 square kilometres, there’s plenty of space for the elephant population to continue to grow.&nbsp;

33
00:05:34,548 --> 00:05:48,837
Their presence is also helping to rebalance Nkhotakota’s damaged ecosystem and providing a sustainable conservation model, which could be replicated in other parks.

34
00:05:48,837 --> 00:06:03,499
[Q40答案句： big draw = increase] All this has been a big draw for tourism, which contributes five times more than the illegal wildlife trade to GDP, and this is mainly because of the elephants.

35
00:06:03,499 --> 00:06:05,999
There’s also been a dramatic rise in interest …

